    "result_text": "Clean build is green. Let me summarize how I addressed the review.\n\n## Response to the review\n\nBoth findings were valid; I addressed both.\n\n### Finding 1 \u2014 the silent removal of `ensureUniqueHousehold` (agreed, made deliberate)\n\nThe removal *is* required: the new test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName + postcode) with no `sharesHousehold` flag and expects **both** to be created, which is fundamentally incompatible with the 409 duplicate guard. So I took the reviewer's first option \u2014 a deliberate, complete removal rather than a quiet unwiring:\n\n- **Deleted** the now-orphaned `EnsureUniqueHousehold.java`, plus its exception machinery: `DuplicateHouseholdException.java`, `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler.java`, and the escalation YAML.\n- **Fixed every stale cross-reference** so docs and pipeline no longer drift:\n  - `Households` \u2192 now points at the new `members` helper instead of \"the create-time duplicate check\".\n  - `AssignHousehold` \u2192 `sharesHousehold` now described as only affecting `FlagPossibleDuplicate` (its sole remaining consumer).\n  - `FlagPossibleDuplicate` \u2192 dropped the \"hard-duplicate 409 already decided by\u2026\" clause.\n  - `DeleteOwner` \u2192 repointed to `Households#members` / `FlagPossibleDuplicate`.\n  - `OwnerTelephones` / `OwnerEmails` \u2192 removed the \"identity key (see EnsureUniqueHousehold)\" clauses. (These were in fact *already* stale \u2014 the deleted class only ever keyed on lastName+postcode, never telephone/email.)\n- A clean build was needed to clear the stale compiled escalation handler from `target/classes`; `mvn clean test` is green.\n\n### Finding 2 \u2014 triplicated household-member scan (agreed, factored out)\n\nAdded a single `Households.members(householdId, owners)` helper that is now the one definition of \"an owner's household members\" (persisted, non-soft-deleted, matching household id; excludes the not-yet-persisted new owner). `CountHouseholdMembers` and `CapMembershipLevel` now both delegate to it and supply only their own aggregation (count+1, and max-level+1). This also **resolves the divergence** the reviewer flagged: `CountHouseholdMembers` previously counted soft-deleted owners; it now skips them, consistent with the documented principle that \"a soft-deleted owner no longer occupies its household.\" No test exercises deleted household members, and the acceptance scenario has none, so behaviour under test is unchanged.\n\n### On the minor note\nI kept `membershipLevelCap` as a real column. The alternative (recomputing the cap at read time) isn't viable here: `getMembershipLevel()` is a pure entity-derived getter with no repository access, and the GET endpoint reloads the owner fresh, so the cap has to be persisted to survive the round-trip. I left it as-is.\n\nFinal: `./mvnw clean test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test, 0 failures.",
