    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\n**1. The diff silently removes the `ensureUniqueHousehold` step \u2014 this is not additive and is unrelated to the spec.**\nThe spec only asks to cap `membershipLevel`. Yet `owners.POST.yml` drops the entire `ensureUniqueHousehold` step (the 409 `DuplicateHouseholdException` guard). Consequences a maintainer would find jarring:\n- `EnsureUniqueHousehold.java` is left in the tree but is now **orphaned** \u2014 no YAML wires it, so it's dead code with no indication why.\n- Several javadocs now describe behavior that no longer runs: `Households` (\"used ... when the create-time duplicate check (`EnsureUniqueHousehold`) decides...\"), `AssignHousehold` (\"it only bypasses the create-time duplicate block\"), and `FlagPossibleDuplicate` all still reference an active duplicate check. The documentation and the pipeline have drifted apart.\n\nIf removing the uniqueness check is genuinely required for this feature, that's a deliberate behavioral change that deserves its own treatment (delete the class and the `DuplicateHouseholdException` handler, fix the cross-references), not a quiet unwiring buried in a \"cap membership level\" change. If it is *not* required, it should not be touched at all. Either way, it shouldn't ride along silently here.\n\n**2. `CapMembershipLevel` duplicates the household-membership scan that already lives in two other functions.**\nThe loop `for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) { skip null id; skip deleted; match Households.householdId(existing.getLastName(), existing.getPostcode()) }` is now the third near-identical copy, alongside `CountHouseholdMembers` and `EnsureUniqueHousehold`. The \"owners in the same household as this owner\" predicate is a single concept and should be factored into one place (e.g. a helper on `Households` returning the household members, or the predicate). As it stands the three copies have already diverged \u2014 `CountHouseholdMembers` does *not* skip soft-deleted owners while `CapMembershipLevel` and `EnsureUniqueHousehold` do \u2014 which is exactly the maintenance hazard duplicated logic creates. Reusing a shared member-selection helper would keep the household definition in one spot (the stated role of `Households`) and let each function supply only its own aggregation.\n\nMinor: persisting `membershipLevelCap` as a real column to feed the `@Transient` `getMembershipLevel()` is slightly awkward (a one-time, create-pipeline value stored permanently on an entity whose level is otherwise fully derived), but the `bandMembershipLevel` extraction in `Owner` is clean and the function's placement/naming otherwise fit the pipeline conventions.",
